Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. BEDROOM - NIGHT

A modest shuttered bedroom. THE NARRATOR, a lean man in a dark coat, stands in the doorway holding a closed lantern.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

THE NARRATOR steps inside, opens the lantern a crack, and directs a thin ray onto the bed where THE OLD MAN lies asleep. The ray falls on a pale blue vulture eye filmed over.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

THE NARRATOR withdraws, closes the lantern, and exits silently.

INT. BEDROOM - NIGHT

Seven nights pass in quick succession. Each midnight THE NARRATOR enters with the same caution, thrusts his head through the door, opens the lantern, and finds the eye closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

Each morning THE NARRATOR enters boldly and speaks to THE OLD MAN.

NARRATOR
Good morning. How did you pass the night?

INT. BEDROOM - NIGHT

On the eighth night THE NARRATOR pushes the door open even more slowly. THE OLD MAN sits up.

OLD MAN
Who’s there?

THE NARRATOR freezes. An hour passes in silence. A low groan rises from the bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

THE NARRATOR opens the lantern a hair’s breadth. The ray falls full on the wide-open vulture eye. A low, dull, quick sound like a watch wrapped in cotton begins—the beating of THE OLD MAN’S heart.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It grew louder—louder—louder! I thought the heart must burst.

THE NARRATOR flings the lantern wide, leaps forward with a yell, drags THE OLD MAN to the floor, and pulls the heavy bed over him. After many minutes the muffled heartbeat stops.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

THE NARRATOR dismembers the body, lifts three floor planks, places the pieces between the joists, replaces the boards, and smiles.

INT. HALLWAY - NIGHT

Four o’clock. A knock at the street door. THE NARRATOR opens it, smiling, to three police officers.

NARRATOR
Gentlemen, the shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Search—search well.

THE NARRATOR leads them into the bedroom, sets chairs for them, and sits upon the very planks that hide the corpse. The officers chat pleasantly. A low, dull, quick sound begins again—louder, louder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It grew louder—louder—louder! And still the men chatted pleasantly, and smiled.

THE NARRATOR springs up, paces, foams, raves, swings the chair against the floorboards.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

FADE OUT.
